# Java modules — the 60-second tour

Java 9's **module system** (JPMS) draws a real boundary around a group of packages — beyond what `public` ever gave you.

## `module-info.java`

```java
module com.acme.orders {
    requires com.acme.payments;   // dependency
    exports com.acme.orders.api;  // outsiders see THIS package…
    // …every other package stays hidden, even public classes
}
```

A package you don't `export` is **inaccessible outside — even its `public` classes.**

## What modules buy you

- **Strong encapsulation** — internal packages genuinely hidden; `public` stops meaning "global"
- **Reliable configuration** — `requires` graph checked **at startup**, not at a runtime `NoClassDefFoundError` (no more classpath hell)

## The JDK is modular

`java.base`, `java.sql`, `java.xml`… enabling **`jlink`** — a custom runtime image with only the modules you use (smaller, faster start).

## Scope

Most apps still use the **classpath** and are fine. Modules matter for the **platform** + large systems. Goal for now: **recognize** `module-info.java`.
